## Development

Bootstrap the selected {{ installer }} environment and run the tests:

    mise run install
    mise run sync
    mise run test

Run `mise tasks` for the full task list and `mise run quality` for Ruff lint,
format checks, and {{ type_checker }} type checking. Use `mise run test:all`
for complete-suite verification and `mise run test:flaky` to investigate
nondeterminism with two independent test runs. `mise run install` registers
the prek pre-commit and pre-push gates; the agentic layer adds tools and
agent wiring.

### Dependency locking

`pylock.toml` is the standardized PEP 751 lockfile. Commit it to version
control. Run `mise run lock` after changing dependency declarations, then
`mise run sync` to install them.

{% if installer == 'uv' %}
uv keeps `uv.lock` as its native source lock; commit both it and the exported
`pylock.toml`. The lock task writes both files. The sync task installs from
`uv.lock` without exporting or resolving dependencies.
{% elif installer == 'poetry' %}
Poetry keeps `poetry.lock` as its native source lock; commit both it and the
exported `pylock.toml`. Poetry 2.3+ and poetry-plugin-export 1.10+ are required
and declared in `pyproject.toml`.
{% elif installer == 'pip' %}
pip writes `pylock.toml` directly. Its lockfile support is experimental and
the lock is guaranteed only for the Python version and platform that created
it. pip 26.1+ is required. The editable-development sync disables hash
enforcement because pip cannot hash the local editable project; the lockfile
still records hashes for external packages.
{% else %}
pip-compile writes `requirements.txt` as an intermediate, then `pip lock`
converts it to `pylock.toml`. Commit both files. pip's lockfile support is
experimental and the final lock is guaranteed only for the Python version and
platform that created it. pip 26.1+ and pip-tools 7.6+ are required.
{% endif %}

